There was nothing to change in this run, so I made no commit and the issue stays open. I've stopped the loop by adding `needs-decision` and asking how this issue should wait for Debian.

- **Re-scan (about 14:30Z):** `debian:trixie-slim` still resolves to the pinned digest `sha256:a72b4433…`. None of the 46 HIGH findings has a fixed version yet. The only fixable HIGH in the base is zlib1g CVE-2026-85091, which #62 already covers. The build report of 82098cb has 0 findings failing the policy. No one has commented with new direction since Jody's 10:05Z decision.
- **Why I asked:** the pin is already on main (3b92f5f). Under Jody's decision nothing more can happen until Debian ships a fix. Because the issue is still approved and not blocked, the looper has started a new run of it about every 3 minutes since 13:47Z, more than 10 runs that each found nothing to do. It waits on Debian, not on an issue or a pipeline, so `Blocked-by:` and `--wait-on` can't hold it.
- **What I recommended** ([comment](https://github.com/tacplus-dev/llmproxy/issues/63#issuecomment-6950883154)):
  1. Close the issue now, since the pin and the `image-base` gate are on main. When Debian ships the fix, the nightly scan turns these findings blocking and files them as a new issue, which becomes the digest move and the patch release.
  2. Or keep it open but parked until the nightly scan reports a fixed version, then re-approve it.
- **Checkpoint:** pushed to `looper/llmproxy-63`. It carries only the looper's run records (`.looper/`).
- **Left after the decision:** move the digest once a fix exists, land it, cut the patch on release/0.1, and close the issue. There is still no exception, per Jody.

No service handlers were touched, so the trace-propagation check (rule 13) didn't apply.
